  {"type": "split", "h2": "Can your old ducts carry a heat pump? What we check first", "photo": "in", "reverse": True,
   "html": "<p>A hybrid system pairs a cold-climate heat pump with a gas furnace. The heat pump handles cooling and most of the heating, and the furnace takes over in deep cold. It is often the best way to bring an older home's gas bill down without a full rebuild.</p>"
           "<p>The catch is airflow. Heat pumps move more air per ton than the furnaces many older houses started with, and tired ducts can be the bottleneck. Skip the checks and you get noise, weak heat and a system that never reaches its rating. So before we quote, our licensed refrigeration techs look at:</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Static pressure across the existing duct system</li><li>Supply and return sizes against the airflow the heat pump needs</li><li>Whether the new coil fits the plenum above the furnace</li><li>Panel space for a 240V circuit</li><li>An outdoor spot clear of roof drip lines and the neighbour's window</li></ul>"},
